{"step_index":151,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:09:44Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-24T00:39:44+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-24T00:39:45+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/brain/7be09992-af56-4486-bcae-bcfc2545af88/implementation_plan.md`\nTotal Lines: 54\nTotal Bytes: 2557\nShowing lines 1 to 54\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Space Debris and Collision Avoidance Prototype\n2: \n3: This project is a web prototype that ingests satellite TLE (Two-Line Element) data, calculates potential close approaches between objects using the `sgp4` Python library, and displays a risk-ranked dashboard on the frontend. We will use a small dataset to ensure performance and smooth operation for the hackathon prototype.\n4: \n5: ## User Review Required\n6: \n7: - **Dataset**: We will start with a small, hardcoded set of TLEs (or fetch a small subset from Celestrak) for the prototype to keep calculations fast without needing a background task queue. Is this acceptable?\n8: - **Risk Metric**: The primary risk metric will be the closest distance (in kilometers) between two objects over a given time window (e.g., the next 24 hours). \n9: \n10: ## Open Questions\n11: \n12: - Do you have a specific time window in mind for predicting close approaches (e.g., next 12 hours, 24 hours)?\n13: - Should we include any specific visual elements in the dashboard, such as color-coding based on the risk level (e.g., Red for < 5km, Yellow for < 10km)?\n14: \n15: ## Proposed Changes\n16: \n17: We will create a new workspace for this project inside the scratch directory.\n18: \n19: ### Backend (Python/FastAPI)\n20: \n21: #### [NEW] backend/requirements.txt\n22: - Dependencies for the backend: `fastapi`, `uvicorn`, and `sgp4`.\n23: \n24: #### [NEW] backend/main.py\n25: - The core FastAPI application.\n26: - `GET /api/conjunctions`: An endpoint that triggers the calculation and returns a list of predicted close approaches.\n27: \n28: #### [NEW] backend/sgp4_calc.py\n29: - Utility functions to parse TLE data.\n30: - Logic to calculate orbital positions at specific time intervals.\n31: - Algorithm to compare positions of multiple objects and find minimum distances.\n32: \n33: ### Frontend (HTML/JS/Tailwind)\n34: \n35: #### [NEW] frontend/index.html\n36: - The main HTML file for the dashboard.\n37: - Includes Tailwind CSS via CDN for rapid styling.\n38: - Structure for a clean, modern data table or card grid.\n39: \n40: #### [NEW] frontend/app.js\n41: - Vanilla JavaScript to fetch data from the FastAPI backend (`/api/conjunctions`).\n42: - Logic to dynamically populate the dashboard with the calculated risk data.\n43: \n44: #### [NEW] frontend/style.css\n45: - Any minimal custom styles if Tailwind utilities are insufficient (though mostly we'll stick to Tailwind).\n46: \n47: ## Verification Plan\n48: \n49: ### Manual Verification\n50: 1. Start the FastAPI backend server using `uvicorn`.\n51: 2. Open the frontend `index.html` in a web browser.\n52: 3. Verify that the dashboard successfully fetches data from the backend and displays the ranked space debris risks correctly.\n53: 4. Check that the UI looks modern and responsive.\n54: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":152,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:09:45Z","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the most specialized tool available for each specific task to avoid unnecessary complexity. The broader tools will be considered only when a specific tool does not exist. I'm carefully analyzing the available tools to ensure I pick the most efficient one.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":true,"Summary":"Updated implementation plan to include the CesiumJS 3D Orbit Viewer integration and necessary backend TLE endpoint.","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# Space Debris and Collision Avoidance Prototype\n\nThis project is a web prototype that ingests satellite TLE (Two-Line Element) data, calculates potential close approaches between objects using the `sgp4` Python library, and displays a risk-ranked dashboard on the frontend. It now also features a Mission Risk Calculator based on Poisson distribution physics.\n\n## User Review Required\n\n- **CesiumJS Integration**: Adding CesiumJS is a significant frontend addition. We will load CesiumJS and Satellite.js via CDN. Is this acceptable?\n- **3D Globe Interaction**: When a row in the \"Live Tracking\" table is clicked, the globe will pan to that satellite's real-time computed coordinate. Are there specific camera angles or zoom levels you'd prefer?\n\n## Proposed Changes\n\n### Backend (Python/FastAPI)\n\n#### [MODIFY] backend/main.py\n- Add `GET /api/tles` endpoint to expose the raw TLE dataset to the frontend so Cesium can compute real-time positions.\n\n#### [MODIFY] backend/sgp4_calc.py\n- Expose the raw TLE dictionary securely so `main.py` can import and serve it.\n\n### Frontend (HTML/JS/Tailwind)\n\n#### [MODIFY] frontend/index.html\n- Import `CesiumJS` and `satellite.js` via CDNs.\n- Add a new \"3D ORBIT VIEWER\" tab to the main navigation.\n- Add a `<div id=\"cesiumContainer\">` within a new view tab.\n\n#### [MODIFY] frontend/app.js\n- Add logic to initialize the Cesium 3D globe.\n- Fetch TLEs from `/api/tles` and use `satellite.js` to compute real-time `[longitude, latitude, altitude]` coordinates.\n- Render each satellite as a glowing PointGraphic (dot) on the globe.\n- Add click event listeners to the \"Live Tracking\" table rows. When a row is clicked, trigger `viewer.camera.flyTo()` to the satellite's current coordinates.\n\n## Verification Plan\n\n### Manual Verification\n1. Start the FastAPI backend server.\n2. Open the frontend and click on the \"3D ORBIT VIEWER\" tab. Verify that the globe loads and renders the glowing satellite dots.\n3. Switch to the \"LIVE TRACKING\" tab and click on a table row. Verify that it seamlessly switches to the 3D globe and flies the camera to the target object.\n","Description":"Updated implementation plan to outline the CesiumJS 3D integration.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"/Users/anandgopaltiwary/.gemini/antigravity/brain/7be09992-af56-4486-bcae-bcfc2545af88/implementation_plan.md","toolAction":"Updating implementation plan","toolSummary":"Implementation plan update"}}]}
