{"step_index":171,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:11:52Z","content":"I have successfully integrated the `CesiumJS` 3D Orbit Viewer! \n\nHere is what I've implemented:\n1. **New Backend Endpoint**: I added a `GET /api/tles` endpoint to `main.py` that securely exposes the raw Two-Line Elements (TLEs) data from our active tracking catalog.\n2. **3D Orbit Viewer Tab**: Added a new tab to the navigation called **3D ORBIT VIEWER** that contains a full-screen interactive 3D globe.\n3. **Real-time Satellite Plotting**: Using `satellite.js` in the frontend, the app fetches the TLEs and computes their precise, real-time geodetic coordinates (longitude, latitude, altitude), and then plots them on the Cesium Earth as glowing cyan dots with labels.\n4. **Interactive `flyTo` Panning**: I linked the Live Tracking table to the Cesium globe. If you are on the Live Tracking tab and click any row, the app automatically switches to the 3D viewer tab and executes a cinematic `viewer.camera.flyTo()` swoop directly to the primary satellite's current coordinates in space!\n\n(I also made sure the Risk Calculator is now fully wired up to the live Python `POST /api/mission-risk` endpoint we built earlier).\n\nPlease **refresh your browser at http://localhost:3000** to load the Cesium library and try out the new 3D visualizations! Click on the CRITICAL row in the Live Tracking table to watch the camera fly up to its orbit. Let me know what you think of the cinematic design!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the \"view_file\" tool where possible, and only resort to broader commands when absolutely needed. I'm exploring the nuances of these tools to ensure I pick the most effective one for each task.\n\n\n"}
